graph":{"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Hypergraph","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/hypergraph","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Hypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"Hypergraph","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"An undirected hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"set of vertices of any size, the empty set included (Berge, "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Hypergraphs"}]},{"type":"text","text":", 1989). Not a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and no shared parent with one: a hyperedge is not an edge. The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"type":"text","text":" module’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidenceGraph"},{"type":"text","text":" reads a hypergraph as its bipartite incidence graph (the Levi graph), which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"how the graph algorithms reach it."}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Hypergraph","kind":"identifier"}]}}}